<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carthaginian Iberia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">237–<a href="/years/206-bce">206 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carthaginian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> was the territory in southern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcid-family">Barcid family</a> from 237 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a> landed at Gades, until the Roman conquest in 206 BCE. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines and manpower rebuilt Carthaginian power after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a>, its capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a>, and it was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s base for the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 218 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carthaginian-iberia.jpg" alt="Carthaginian Iberia" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dama_de_Elche_(M.A.N._Madrid)_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a>, traders mainly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>, founded many trading colonies around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, including some in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and in 814 BCE founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> on the coast of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a> fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, Carthage became the most powerful Phoenician city and annexed other colonies of the western Mediterranean, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sousse">Hadrumetum</a> and Thapsus, along with territory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. Its province in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> covered the Mediterranean part of the peninsula.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>'s defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a>, the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a> crushed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and trained a new army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numidians">Numidians</a>, mercenaries and other infantry. He led it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> hoping to win a new empire to make up for the territories lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and to serve as a base for revenge. His success began at Gadir, and by force and diplomacy he secured around half of the peninsula and founded the city of Akra Leuke, before dying in battle in 228 BCE. His son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-the-fair">Hasdrubal the Fair</a> carried the conquest to the Tagus River by his death in 221 BCE and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Qart Hadasht</a>, New Town, around 227 BCE.</p><p>The province fell in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. In 209 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">New Carthage</a>, the centre of Punic power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. At the Battle of Baecula they could not stop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-barca">Hasdrubal Barca</a> marching to reinforce his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The defeat of the Carthaginian forces at Ilipa in 206 BCE sealed the province's fate, and Gades, which had already rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In 205 BCE Mago tried to retake New Carthage while the Romans faced a mutiny and an Iberian rising, but was repulsed and sailed for Italy from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Iberian soldiers made up a large part of the army that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> led into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Gadir drew closer to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> soon after the war, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Qart Hadasht</a> became the present-day city of Cartagena. Carthage itself was destroyed in 146 BCE at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-punic-war">Third Punic War</a>, about 50 years after it lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carthaginian Iberia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
